slug":"tony-sheldon","vote":"no"},{"name":"Tyron Whitten","party":"Pauline Hanson's One Nation Party","person_id":"tvfy_11060","person_slug":"tyron-whitten","vote":"aye"},{"name":"Vanessa Bleyer","party":"Greens","person_id":"tvfy_11065","person_slug":"vanessa-bleyer","vote":"no"},{"name":"Varun Ghosh","party":"Labor","person_id":"tvfy_11017","person_slug":"varun-ghosh","vote":"no"}],"name":"Matters of Urgency — Cost of Living","noes":32,"question":"Slade Brockman Senator McGrath has submitted a proposal under standing order 75, today, as shown at item 13 of today's Order of Business: That, in the opinion of the Senate, the following is a matter of urgency: The need to end the Albanese Government's assault on household budgets, with Australians paying more tax, more for their mortgage or rent and more for everyday essentials, while Labor pursues even more taxes that will make it harder to get ahead. Is consideration of the proposal supported? _More than the number of senators required by the standing orders having risen in their places—_ With the concurrence of the Senate, the clerks will set the clock in accordance with informal arrangements made by the whips. James McGrath I move: That, in the opinion of the Senate, the following is a matter of urgency: The need to end the Albanese Government's assault on household budgets, with Australians paying more tax, more for their mortgage or rent and more for everyday essentials, while Labor pursues even more taxes that will make it harder to get ahead. There is a bubble in Canberra, and it seems to be a bit of a left-wing bubble where the Labor Party and their allies in the Greens seem to think that everything is tickety-boo out there in the real world, but it's not. Australians, regardless of the state or territory in which they live and regardless of their individual economic circumstances, are being assaulted daily by the decisions of this Labor government. It is an assault on their aspirations, on their lifestyle and on how Australians want to live from day to day. Under the Labor Party, since they were elected in 2022, taxes have gone up, services have gone up, and yet Australians are doing it tougher because real wages have not increased. Under the Labor Party's economic policies, we're in this vicious cycle where more is being spent, which drives inflation, and then we've got the tax that hits the inflation, and then they spend even more. So the result is an ","result":"negative","slug":"division-federal-senate-10765","source_url":"https://theyvoteforyou.org.au/divisions/senate/2026-09-14/13"}
